= IKEA effect =

== Definition ==
The '''IKEA effect''' is an increase in subjective valuation of an object that a person has assembled or helped create, compared with an otherwise similar object produced by somebody else. The effect concerns ''self-creation and valuation'', not necessarily the objective quality, use value or market price of the object. Liking one's own work is not intrinsically irrational.

== Experimental evidence ==
=== Foundational demonstrations ===
Norton, Mochon and Ariely (2012) reported four studies involving assembled IKEA boxes, folded origami and LEGO constructions. People assigned relatively high values to their own amateur creations and sometimes expected other people to share those valuations. Successful completion mattered: tasks left unfinished or creations subsequently destroyed substantially weakened or removed the measured effect in those experiments.<ref name="norton">[https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcps.2011.08.002 Norton, M. I., Mochon, D. & Ariely, D. (2012), "The IKEA effect: When labor leads to love", ''Journal of Consumer Psychology'' 22(3):453–460.]</ref>

=== Mechanistic and developmental studies ===
Mochon, Norton and Ariely (2012) found evidence implicating feelings of competence. Self-affirmation reduced valuation of self-made items in a tested experimental context, while self-threat increased willingness to engage in creation; this is not a validated universal intervention.<ref name="competence">[https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijresmar.2012.05.001 Mochon, D., Norton, M. I. & Ariely, D. (2012), "Bolstering and restoring feelings of competence via the IKEA effect", ''International Journal of Research in Marketing'' 29(4):363–369.]</ref>

Koster and colleagues (2015) reported that beliefs about authorship, including experimentally induced false beliefs about self-creation, changed valuation. Associated functional-imaging patterns do not uniquely establish a causal neural mechanism.<ref name="koster">[https://doi.org/10.3389/fnhum.2015.00473 Koster, R. et al. (2015), "How beliefs about self-creation inflate value in the human brain", ''Frontiers in Human Neuroscience'' 9:473.]</ref>

Marsh, Kanngiesser and Hood (2018) observed emergence of the effect around age five in their developmental tasks. Manipulated effort and simple ownership did not account for the observed pattern in their particular samples; a self-concept explanation remains an interpretation to be tested more broadly.<ref name="marsh">[https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cognition.2017.10.012 Marsh, L. E., Kanngiesser, P. & Hood, B. (2018), "When and how does labour lead to love?", ''Cognition'' 170:245–253.]</ref>

=== Quantitative synthesis ===
Pelled, Demetriades and Walter (online 2025; issue 2026) synthesized 55 study effects with a combined N of 5,454 and reported a pooled standardized effect size of ''d'' = 0.57 (''p'' < 0.005). The result is a moderate standardized effect, '''not a 57% increase in willingness to pay'''. A meta-analytic average does not establish identical effects for every task or context; sampling, study heterogeneity, publication bias and measurement choices remain limitations of inference.<ref name="meta">[https://doi.org/10.1002/mar.70064 Pelled, A., Demetriades, S. Z. & Walter, N. (first published 2025), "Labor Leads to Love, Right? A Meta-Analysis of the IKEA Effect", ''Psychology & Marketing'' 43(2):388–400 (2026).]</ref>

== Mechanisms: evidence versus interpretations ==
