== Distinguish neighboring phenomena ==
* [[Sunk cost effect]] concerns the inappropriate weight placed on irreversible past investments when deciding what to do next; the IKEA effect concerns valuation of what one made.
* [[Confirmation bias]] preferentially processes evidence consistent with beliefs; it need not involve authorship.
* [[Overconfidence effect]] concerns confidence or estimation accuracy; the IKEA effect can occur without inaccurate claims about measurable technical performance.
* [[Loss aversion]] concerns the asymmetry of gains and losses relative to a reference point, not creation alone.
* The endowment effect concerns the effect of possession or ownership on valuation. Ownership and self-creation can interact, but are not synonyms.

== Value is multidimensional ==
'''Personal significance''' is what a creation means to its creator. '''Instrumental utility''' concerns fitness for a defined purpose. '''Exchange value''' concerns what others will pay or trade. These can diverge legitimately. A creator's attachment only becomes a decision error under a specified objective—for example, choosing an inferior product for a shared task while treating private attachment as publicly verifiable quality.

== Evaluation and debiasing ==
The distinction between evidence from direct IKEA-effect experiments and broader decision-process safeguards matters.

'''Directly tested in specific experiments:''' self-affirmation attenuated overvaluation in a tested context.<ref name="competence"/> It should not be marketed as a universal cure.

'''Practical decision safeguards, not established IKEA-effect treatments:'''
# Record personal meaning independently of task performance and external willingness to pay.
# Compare self-built and external alternatives using identical, predeclared functional criteria.
# Where feasible, request authorship-blinded evaluations from independent reviewers.
# Rely on observed outcomes—task success, usability, reliability, adoption—rather than on creator confidence alone.
# Exclude unrecoverable past costs when deciding whether to invest further; use expected future costs and benefits.
# Precommit to continue, revise or discontinue thresholds; document contrary evidence.

These are procedural proposals informed by general decision hygiene, '''not''' a validated diagnostic or intervention scale for the IKEA effect.

== Possible applications and limits ==
In software development, [[AI agent]] evaluation, academic research, [[Engineering]], manufacturing and entrepreneurship, creators may value their own designs highly. The specific consumer-goods experimental results '''cannot simply be transferred''' to all those settings; actual effects require context-specific measurement. [[Reproducibility]] and [[Validation]] offer methods for comparing claims of functional success.

== Open questions ==
How far does the effect generalize to collaborative or AI-assisted production, long-lived software, scientific theories and organizational designs? How do self-authorship, expertise, incentives and objective output quality interact? Which safeguards actually reduce consequential decision errors without destroying legitimate personal value?

